# Fleet — Environment Variables Example
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values.
# setup.sh creates .env at ./fleet/.env (not the repo root) — a gitignored
# subdir under the repo root that holds all runtime state.
# Never commit any copy of .env to version control.
#
# Note: FLEET_BASE_DIR is set automatically by setup.sh to ./fleet under the
# repo root. You don't need to configure it manually.

# ── CTO agent ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Short name of your co-cto agent (created interactively via ./setup.sh).
# Seed-shipped workflow definitions reference this via {{config.CtoAgent}} so
# they target your agent without hardcoding a name. Also used as the escalation
# target for workflow failure notifications.
FLEET_CTO_AGENT=

# Optional: Telegram group chat ID for fleet conversation visibility.
# Create a Telegram group, add every fleet bot (notifier + CTO) as members,
# then forward any message from the group to @userinfobot — it replies with
# the group's negative integer ID. Agents use this group to post status
# updates, workflow notifications, and cross-agent coordination messages.
# Leave blank to disable group routing (bots only respond to DMs) —
# docker-compose defaults it to 0 when unset.
FLEET_GROUP_CHAT_ID=

# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FLEET_MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=changeme
FLEET_MYSQL_PASSWORD=fleetpass

# ── Telegram bot tokens ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Create bots via @BotFather on Telegram. You need two bots:
# TELEGRAM_NOTIFIER_BOT_TOKEN — shared bot used by fleet-bridge and worker agents
#                               (developer, etc.) for DMs and group-chat relay
# TELEGRAM_CTO_BOT_TOKEN      — dedicated bot for the CTO agent's DMs with you
#                               (can reuse the same token if running only one bot)
TELEGRAM_NOTIFIER_BOT_TOKEN=654321:XYZ-GHI5678jkLmn-abc90W3w4x456ew22
TELEGRAM_CTO_BOT_TOKEN=

# Your Telegram numeric user ID — every agent you provision gets this ID added to
# its allowedTelegramUsers automatically so you can DM it immediately.
# To find your ID: send any message to @userinfobot on Telegram.
TELEGRAM_USER_ID=

# ── GitHub App ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Create a GitHub App at github.com/settings/apps.
# Grant it repo read/write access and install it on the target org/account.
# GITHUB_APP_PEM: base64-encoded private key — run: base64 -w0 your-app.pem
GITHUB_APP_ID=123456
GITHUB_APP_PEM=base64-encoded-private-key-here

# ── Orchestrator auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Protects mutating REST endpoints on the orchestrator (POST/PUT/DELETE).
# Generate a random secret, e.g.: openssl rand -hex 32
ORCHESTRATOR_AUTH_TOKEN=your-secret-token-here

# Separate token for /api/config/* endpoints consumed by peer services.
# Peer containers (fleet-telegram, fleet-bridge, fleet-temporal-bridge) use this
# token to bootstrap their config on startup and to receive config.changed events.
# Generate a random secret, e.g.: openssl rand -hex 32
ORCHESTRATOR_CONFIG_TOKEN=your-config-token-here

# ── MinIO (S3-compatible file sharing between agents) ─────────────────────────
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin

# ── Fleet Memory embedding ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Embedding backend for fleet-memory. Options: onnx (default) or ollama.
# onnx runs inside the container with zero external deps.
# ollama requires ollama running on the host with a pulled model.
FLEET_MEMORY_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=onnx
FLEET_MEMORY_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=384
# Only needed when FLEET_MEMORY_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=ollama:
# FLEET_MEMORY_OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
# FLEET_MEMORY_OLLAMA_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# Embedding-input size guidance in UTF-8 bytes (title + content of one memory).
# Empty or 0 = off (the default). Advisory: writes still succeed. Starting points:
# onnx/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (512 tokens, hard-cut) about 2000; ollama/bge-m3 25000.
# Read once at startup: recreate fleet-memory after changing it.
# FLEET_MEMORY_EMBEDDING_INPUT_GUIDANCE_BYTES=

# ── Prompt size warnings (orchestrator) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Soft limits in UTF-8 bytes for one role instruction and one project context,
# both loaded into every assigned agent's prompt on every turn. Default 10000
# each; 0 = off. Advisory: over-limit writes are saved and reported. Read once
# at startup: recreate the orchestrator after changing either.
# PromptSizeWarnings__InstructionBytes=10000
# PromptSizeWarnings__ProjectContextBytes=10000

# ── Auth token refresh (OAuth client IDs) ────────────────────────────────────
# Used by AuthTokenRefreshWorkflow to rotate Claude/Codex access tokens from
# their refresh tokens. These are per-application constants — same for every
# user of the Claude/Codex CLIs — and identify the CLI application, not your
# account. They are not secrets, so they ship with known defaults; override
# only if Anthropic/OpenAI rotate them.
AUTHTOKENREFRESH__CLAUDECLIENTID=9d1c250a-e61b-44d9-88ed-5944d1962f5e
AUTHTOKENREFRESH__CODEXCLIENTID=app_EMoamEEZ73f0CkXaXp7hrann

# ── Whisper (speech-to-text) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional decoding glossary for the transcription service: domain terms the
# model otherwise mis-hears, such as project or product names. Supply them as
# one plain string, e.g. "Acme Widgets, Foobar".
# Hotwords bias decoding toward these terms — they do not guarantee exact
# spelling. Leave empty to keep the default decode behaviour.
# The glossary is deployment-specific; keep your own vocabulary out of git.
WHISPER_HOTWORDS=

# ── Codex provider — local models (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# Only needed for a codex agent whose Model carries an `ollama/` or `lmstudio/`
# prefix, e.g. `ollama/gpt-oss:20b`. The prefix selects the codex built-in
# modelProvider; this is the OpenAI-compatible base URL it talks to.
#
# There is no default on purpose. codex would otherwise resolve localhost, which
# inside an agent container is the container itself — so an agent with a prefixed
# model and no value here fails startup instead of coming up pointed at nothing.
#
# From a container, the host's inference port is host.docker.internal.
# Attach the key to the agent as an Env Ref and reprovision — it is read at
# process start, not live-mutable.
# Full guide: docs/providers/codex-local-models.md
#CODEX_OSS_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1

# GLM for the codex provider on the Z.ai GLM Coding Plan (optional). A codex
# agent whose Model is `zai/glm-5.3` runs through a loopback forwarder inside
# the agent, on your Coding Plan subscription. Attach this key to that agent as
# an Env Ref, then reprovision.
#
# ⚠️ Data flow: the agent's prompts, tool results and MCP output go to Z.ai.
# Enable it only for agents whose data you may share with Z.ai.
#
# ⚠️ Plan terms: the quota is for the subscriber's personal use. Never give a
# zai/ model to an agent that answers other people (household members,
# customers, shared group chats).
#
# This name is reserved for hosted routing: entrypoint.sh unsets it on every
# agent, so a non-hosted agent never sees it. The key never reaches codex or
# the model's shell. Full guide: docs/providers/codex-hosted-models.md
#ZAI_CODING_PLAN_API_KEY=

# ── Gemini provider ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Gemini agents use OAuth credentials, not an API key.
# Run 'gemini auth' once on the host to produce ~/.gemini/oauth_creds.json,
# then run ./setup.sh (choose option 3 or 5) to copy credentials to the fleet dir.
# The credentials file is mounted read-write into gemini agent containers so the
# CLI's google-auth-library can refresh tokens in-place.
# No GEMINI_API_KEY or GOOGLE_AI_STUDIO_KEY is required or used.

# ── Fleet.Comms — first-party client API (opt-in) ────────────────────────────
# Auth and session only: device enrollment, token mint, self-revocation, session
# discovery. No chat, no WebSocket, no conversation history, no push.
# Full guide: docs/comms-deployment.md
#
# Commented out ON PURPOSE. setup.sh asks the question only when the value is
# absent, so shipping `FLEET_COMMS_ENABLED=false` here would copy a decision into
# every fresh .env and the prompt would never fire — "answer y" with no question
# to answer. Absent means unchosen; setup.sh writes false when you decline.
#FLEET_COMMS_ENABLED=
#
# Where the client API publishes. LOOPBACK by default, on purpose: this endpoint
# takes credentials, and a host reverse proxy terminating TLS can reach it here
# while the LAN and the internet cannot. Change it only once TLS is in front.
# Without TLS, enrollment codes, device secrets and tokens travel in clear text.
#FLEET_COMMS_BIND=127.0.0.1:3500
#
# Trust X-Forwarded-For from the immediate peer. Set true ONLY when a reverse
# proxy is actually in front AND replaces the header rather than appending to it
# (nginx: `proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;` — never
# $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for). On a port reachable without a proxy, true lets any
# caller choose the rate limiter's partition key and mint a fresh budget.
# false is degraded-but-bounded behind a proxy: one shared limiter bucket.
#FLEET_COMMS_TRUST_PROXY=false
#
# Written by setup.sh/upgrade.sh after the auth store is first initialised. Do
# not set it by hand: it is how `store init` tells a first install from a lost
# volume, and claiming a store exists when it does not blocks the one command
# that could create one.
#FLEET_COMMS_STORE_PROVISIONED=
#
# Non-secret display label returned by GET /v1/session.
#FLEET_COMMS_AGENT_LABEL=assistant

# ── Fleet.Comms — durable conversations (opt-in, on top of the above) ────────
# Adds conversation open, catch-up, submissions, cancel, cursor and the
# WebSocket stream, backed by MySQL and a broker. Leaving these absent keeps the
# auth-only surface EXACTLY as it is: the six routes are not mapped, no database
# connection is attempted, and no background loop runs.
#
# ⚠️ TWO DATABASE ACCOUNTS, and the split is the point. The service runs as an
# account with SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE and NO DDL grants; migrations use a
# separate account that has them. That is what makes "the service never migrates
# on startup" a property of the deployment rather than of the code being careful
# — a process that tried would be refused by the database.
#
# The presence of the RUNTIME string is what enables the feature.
#FLEET_COMMS_CONVERSATION_DB=Server=comms-mysql;Port=3306;Database=comms;User ID=comms_runtime;Password=CHANGE_ME;
#
# DDL account, used ONLY by `conversations migrate`. Give it to the operator, not
# to the running container: a runtime account that can alter the schema turns a
# bug into a migration and removes the guard that would have caught it.
#FLEET_COMMS_CONVERSATION_MIGRATION_DB=Server=comms-mysql;Port=3306;Database=comms;User ID=comms_ddl;Password=CHANGE_ME;
#
# The agent-facing SOUTH listener. Container-internal: it is reached by a peer
# container on the same Docker network and is NEVER published as a host port and
# NEVER proxied. It carries an administrative surface — a caller who reached
# /turns:commit could write a terminal for someone else's turn.
# Its rule is the OPPOSITE of the ops listener's: ops is loopback because only
# this container's healthcheck calls it, and a loopback bind here would make the
# surface unreachable by construction.
#FLEET_COMMS_SOUTH_BIND=http://0.0.0.0:8082
#
# Bearer credential the south listener requires. No default; the process refuses
# to start without it when conversations are enabled. Generate one per
# deployment: `openssl rand -base64 32`.
#FLEET_COMMS_SOUTH_TOKEN=
#
# The agent commands are routed to. No default; a blank value fails startup.
# It becomes a routing key AND a queue-name segment, so it is validated as
# [A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128} — a dot or a slash would produce a queue name the
# consumer cannot address.
#FLEET_COMMS_AGENT_NAME=
#
# Broker for the two outboxes. Without it the outboxes still accumulate
# correctly and nothing is lost — a client's submission is durable before it is
# ever published — but nothing is dispatched until a broker is configured.
#FLEET_COMMS_BROKER=amqp://guest:guest@rabbitmq:5672/
#
# The conversation database container. Used once, on first start, to provision the
# two accounts above — nothing reads it afterwards, and no service runs as root.
# Generate one: `openssl rand -base64 24`.
#FLEET_COMMS_MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=
#FLEET_COMMS_MYSQL_DATABASE=comms
#
# The two account passwords, read by the init script on the database's FIRST start
# and never afterwards. They must match the two connection strings above.
#
# They live here and in no tracked file. A password committed to a public
# repository is a password every checkout holds; a placeholder committed there is
# a password every deployment that did not notice it holds.
# Generate each: `openssl rand -base64 24`.
#FLEET_COMMS_MYSQL_DDL_PASSWORD=
#FLEET_COMMS_MYSQL_RUNTIME_PASSWORD=
#
# ⚠️ Claim retention MUST exceed the broker's redelivery horizon, and also the
# longest a submission can legitimately wait behind a running turn. A `done`
# claim inside its retention is the ONLY thing standing between a redelivered
# command and a duplicate turn — the attempt state machine refuses a SECOND
# start of a running attempt, not a first one. Shortening this removes the
# guard rather than tightening it.
#FLEET_COMMS_CLAIM_RETENTION=7.00:00:00
#
# Remaining retention horizons, each operator-settable. Defaults shown.
# These are a garbage-collection horizon, NOT a deletion feature.
#FLEET_COMMS_EPHEMERAL_RETENTION=1.00:00:00
#FLEET_COMMS_DURABLE_RETENTION=30.00:00:00
#FLEET_COMMS_OUTBOX_RETENTION=1.00:00:00
